Z||000000||fierovette||06-21-2001||06:47 PM||||Pressure is pegged at max, wsa told its the sensor? cant locate it on my v6. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||varnster||06-21-2001||09:40 PM||||it is located on right side of the engine, 4<BR>inches to the left of the battery holder. it<BR>has 3 wires coming out of the top, pull this conection off pull the battery get the appropriate wrenches {2} put the wrenches on the fitting located on the BOTTOM ,DONT WRENCH ON THE PLASTIC! the sensor is mounted on a flimsey bracket that you will destroy if<BR>you dont put a wrench on it while turning the<BR>sensor. If it is stuck get a heat gun and apply to the bottom of the sensor this will <BR>melt the locktight substance applied to the<BR>fitting from the factory. good luck!!||||reg||6
Z||000002||LarryB||06-21-2001||09:51 PM||||Here are a couple of pics showing where it's located on my '86 SE V6 (4-speed).  It's at the same spot on my 5 speed '87 GT.  I've heard before that non-A/C equipped cars have the sensor mounted on the side facing the front but can't confirm or deny that.  Both my cars have A/C.<P>This first pic is with the old sensor removed; you can see where it mounts just before the "B" on my copyright. <BR> <A HREF="http://fiero.xcom.net/fiero1a.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.xcom.net/fiero1a.jpg</A> <P>This picture is more of a closeup with the new sensor installed.<BR> <A HREF="http://fiero.xcom.net/fiero2a.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.xcom.net/fiero2a.jpg</A> <P>After you've done one you'll be able to do it in about 15 minutes.  Easiest way is to remove the battery, then remove the plastic side of the battery tray, remove the wire harness from the top of the sensor, use a stubby wrench (9/16" from memory) to unscrew the old sensor, put the supplied anti-sieze compound on the threads of the new one, screw the new one in, and reverse the above steps.  The worst part of the whole procedure is removing the (*$&)%$%^#$!!! harness off the old sensor, you have to squeeze hard and twist it off at the same time.<BR>||||reg||1
